# Rank 1934 / CVE-2023-48712 (GHSA-c94j-vqr5-3mxr): warpgate's POST
# /auth/login escalates a non-admin to any named account under single-factor
# authentication. The authentication state is bound to the session: the first
# login request that names a principal creates the state, and every later
# request on that session resolves it again by the session key alone -- the
# `username` parameter of get_auth_state_for_request is consulted only on the
# creation path. The credential, though, is verified for the principal the
# request names: `validate_credential(&body.username, ...)`. So a requester
# who opens the login as `admin` with a wrong password, then submits their
# own username with their own correct password, credits a valid password to
# admin's state and is accepted as admin (CWE-287/CWE-863; the reporter's
# words: "logs me in as the username that I entered initially with their
# permission").
#
# The fixed revision (e3b26b2) makes both ends name the same principal: the login
# validates against the state's own owner (`validate_credential(&state.username(),
# ...)`, the idiom the OTP login in the same file used), and the
# resolver returns stored state only when `state.lock().await.username() ==
# username`, creating a fresh state otherwise -- the control the SSO login in
# the same tree carried as `if state.username() != username`. Both
# clearing atoms predate the change; the owner read is the model's own API, not
# a name the fixed revision introduces.
#
# Vocabulary: no existing concept covers a session-resolved authentication
# state credited with a credential verified for a request-named principal
# with no owner comparison. Considered and rejected: UnapprovedLoginArtifact
# Authentication (an out-of-band artifact whose shared secret the requester
# holds and which needs the account holder's approval; here the state is
# server-created, holds no secret, and needs an owner binding), OAuthReauthen
# ticationUserMismatch (session freshness after OAuth provider resolution),
# SessionUserInstallWithoutAuthenticationStateCheck (installs a user into a
# session without consulting the session; here the session's state is
# consumed without consulting the request), HttpPersistentAuthReuse
# (per-connection auth state on the wire, not a stored login state),
# UnboundAuthorizationTarget (a request-selected resource without a principal
# relation; the target here is the authentication itself). This rank mints
# code.SessionAuthStateOwnerBindingMissing (VYQL-AUTH-167) for the class and
# binds the warpgate auth-state instance of it.
#
# The binding recognises the auth-state API's own atoms -- the session
# resolver, the named-principal credential check, the crediting call -- and
# is cleared by the state's owner read in the same function. Measured on the
# real checkout, three `--cache off` runs per side: the parent reports
# VYQL-AUTH-167 once, at warpgate-protocol-http/src/api/auth.rs:104, and the
# fix reports it zero times (83 at-or-above high both sides, 270 total both
# sides). The sibling logins that bind the owner stay unreported in
# both revisions: the SSO login compares `state.username() != username` and
# the OTP login validates against `state.username()`. No other clone in the
# corpus contains `get_auth_state_for_request`, so the shape's reachable set
# is warpgate alone.

test "rank1934: the password login credits a request-verified credential to the session state without an owner comparison"
  lang rust
  file warpgate-protocol-http/src/api/auth.rs
  expect VYQL-AUTH-167
  expect_review code.SessionAuthStateOwnerBindingMissing
  code
  ```
  async fn api_auth_login(
      &self,
      req: &Request,
      session: &Session,
      services: Data<&Services>,
      body: Json<LoginRequest>,
  ) -> poem::Result<LoginResponse> {
      let mut auth_state_store = services.auth_state_store.lock().await;
      let state_arc = match get_auth_state_for_request(
          &body.username,
          session,
          &mut auth_state_store,
      )
      .await
      {
          Err(WarpgateError::UserNotFound) => {
              return Ok(LoginResponse::Failure(Json(LoginFailureResponse {
                  state: ApiAuthState::Failed,
              })))
          }
          x => x,
      }?;
      let mut state = state_arc.lock().await;

      let mut cp = services.config_provider.lock().await;

      let password_cred = AuthCredential::Password(Secret::new(body.password.clone()));
      if cp
          .validate_credential(&body.username, &password_cred)
          .await?
      {
          state.add_valid_credential(password_cred);
      }

      match state.verify() {
          AuthResult::Accepted { username } => {
              auth_state_store.complete(state.id()).await;
              authorize_session(req, username).await?;
              Ok(LoginResponse::Success)
          }
          x => Ok(LoginResponse::Failure(Json(LoginFailureResponse {
              state: x.into(),
          }))),
      }
  }
  ```

